1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:04,605
Most colour advice is a static chart. You
decide you are a winter, buy a foundation, and

2
00:00:04,605 --> 00:00:09,440
it is the wrong depth for your actual skin,
because nothing ever looked at your face.

3
00:00:09,700 --> 00:00:14,532
Chroma starts from the opposite end. It
measures your face with the YouCam API, then

4
00:00:14,532 --> 00:00:17,350
computes everything else from those
measurements.

5
00:00:19,610 --> 00:00:24,624
This is the deployed app. You give it one
photo. The analysis is a real call to the

6
00:00:24,624 --> 00:00:30,242
YouCam skin tone analysis endpoint. There is a
recorded sample too, labelled as recorded, but

7
00:00:30,242 --> 00:00:31,390
I will run it live.

8
00:00:31,650 --> 00:00:36,989
I choose the live path, pick a photo, and run
the measurement. The photo goes to the YouCam

9
00:00:36,989 --> 00:00:40,450
skin tone analysis API, which returns the
measured colours.

10
00:00:40,710 --> 00:00:46,107
Four colours, straight from the API: skin,
lips, eyes and brows, each with its measured

11
00:00:46,107 --> 00:00:51,566
lightness, chroma and hue. Nothing is averaged
or filled in. The API did not return hair

12
00:00:51,566 --> 00:00:57,088
colour, so the app lists it as missing and
lowers its confidence, rather than inventing a

13
00:00:57,088 --> 00:00:57,988
value.

14
00:01:01,720 --> 00:01:07,531
From those four hex values, Chroma derives a
seasonal profile. Here, Bright Spring. The app

15
00:01:07,531 --> 00:01:13,406
shows its work: every signal that voted on the
undertone, and what each contributed. This is

16
00:01:13,406 --> 00:01:16,280
a styling framework, not a medical diagnosis.

17
00:01:16,540 --> 00:01:22,150
The YouCam makeup engine accepts arbitrary hex
colours, so the looks are not presets. Each is

18
00:01:22,150 --> 00:01:27,398
computed from the measured palette, and the
exact transformation is printed under every

19
00:01:27,398 --> 00:01:28,298
swatch.

20
00:01:32,080 --> 00:01:37,302
Now the try-on. I press render, and the
computed look is sent to the YouCam makeup

21
00:01:37,302 --> 00:01:38,830
virtual try-on endpoint.

22
00:01:39,090 --> 00:01:44,057
That is the composited image: the same photo,
with the look computed from the measured

23
00:01:44,057 --> 00:01:47,580
colours. Nothing is a preset, and nothing is
manually edited.

24
00:01:52,840 --> 00:01:57,649
The same measurement drives the shopping
advice. Any garment colour is scored against

25
00:01:57,649 --> 00:02:02,571
the palette with CIEDE2000, the perceptual
distance the makeup engine uses, and the app

26
00:02:02,571 --> 00:02:04,890
suggests a colour that would work better.

27
00:02:05,150 --> 00:02:09,914
Foundation is matched on depth first,
undertone second, because the wrong depth is

28
00:02:09,914 --> 00:02:12,470
far more visible than slightly wrong warmth.

29
00:02:16,730 --> 00:02:21,896
Measure, interpret, personalize, try on,
compare. One measurement, and every

30
00:02:21,896 --> 00:02:25,770
recommendation traces back to it. That is the
whole idea.

31
00:02:26,030 --> 00:02:29,180
Try it at chroma-r8sd.vercel.app.
